.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt MORE 1
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm more ,
.Nm less
.Nd page through text
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl ceFisu
.Op Fl n Ar number
.Op Fl p Ar command
.Op Fl t Ar pattern
.Op + Ns Ar command
.Op Ar
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility shows text one screen at a time.
It reads each
.Ar file ,
or the standard input.
Invoked as
.Nm less ,
it behaves the same.
When the standard output is not a terminal, the input is copied to it
unchanged.
.Pp
Bold and underline, as escape sequences or as backspace overstrike, are
shown as such; tabs expand to every eighth column, and long lines fold.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl c , Fl R
Accepted for compatibility.
.It Fl e
Exit on reaching the end of the last file.
.It Fl F
Exit at once, after printing it, if the text fits on one screen.
.It Fl i
Ignore case in searches.
.It Fl n Ar number
Show
.Ar number
lines per screen.
.It Fl p Ar command , No + Ns Ar command
Run
.Ar command
first:
.Ql / Ns Ar pattern ,
.Ql G ,
or a line number.
.It Fl s
Show runs of blank lines as one.
.It Fl t Ar pattern
Start at the first line matching
.Ar pattern .
.It Fl u
Show backspaces as they are, with no bold or underline.
.El
.Pp
The commands are single keys, some preceded by a count
.Ar N :
.Bl -tag -width "SPACE f ^F"
.It SPACE f ^F
Forward one screen, or
.Ar N .
At the end of a file, go to the next.
.It b ^B
Back one screen.
.It RETURN j e ^N
Forward one line, or
.Ar N .
.It k y ^Y ^P
Back one line.
.It d ^D , u ^U
Forward or back half a screen.
.It g <
The first line, or line
.Ar N .
.It G >
The last line, or line
.Ar N .
.It / Ns Ar pattern
Search forward for
.Ar pattern ,
a literal string; matches are highlighted.
.It \&? Ns Ar pattern
Search backward.
.It n , N
Repeat the last search, or repeat it the other way.
.It :n , :p
The next or previous file.
.It = ^G
Show the position.
.It r ^L
Redraw the screen.
.It h
Summarize the commands.
.It q Q ZZ
Quit.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr man 1 ,
.Xr oxdoc 1
.Sh STANDARDS
The
.Nm
utility follows
.St -p1003.1-2008 ,
except that patterns are literal strings, not regular expressions.
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
command appeared in
.Bx 3.0 .
This implementation was written for OxideBSD.
